      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the math.\n\nThe Fourier Transform decomposes a signal into its frequency components by representing it as a sum of sinusoidal functions, each with a specific frequency and amplitude. The process involves taking the signal and convolving it with a set of basis functions, known as the Fourier basis functions or the Dirac delta function.\n\nThink of it like a series of narrow, goblin-sized tunnels that the signal passes through, one at a time. Each tunnel corresponds to a specific frequency, and the signal's amplitude at each tunnel is the amplitude of the corresponding frequency component. The Fourier Transform essentially \"tunnels\" the signal through these basis functions, allowing us to extract the individual frequency components.\n\nThe decomposition is done using the following formula:\n\nX(f) = ∫∞ -∞ x(t) e^{-i2πft} dt\n\nwhere X(f) is the Fourier Transform of the signal x(t), f is the frequency, and t is time. This formula represents the convolution of the signal with the Dirac delta function, which is the basis function for the Fourier Transform.\n\nIn simpler terms, the Fourier Transform is a mathematical operation that takes a signal and",
